## E5 — Adaptation, Robustness, Resilience, and Self-Maintenance

### E5.1 Stability, Robustness, Adaptation, and Resilience

These imported Kinds remain distinct:

- **Stability**: persistence of a State or organization under specified perturbations and interval.
- **Robustness**: persistence of function or organization across a defined disturbance range.
- **Adaptation**: change through which fit between a system and its conditions is maintained or increased.
- **Recovery**: an actual Process or transition returning or reorganizing after disruption.
- **Resilience**: Capacity to recover or reorganize after disturbance while retaining defining organization or changing it under a declared criterion.

    NON_EQUIVALENT(Stability, Robustness)
    NON_EQUIVALENT(Robustness, Adaptation)

An actual recovery Occurrence is not equivalent to a Resilience Capacity. Survival is not equivalent to an increase in fitness. These are semantic firewall clauses, not declarations of compressed CamelCase operand aliases.

A system may be robust without adapting, adaptive but not resilient, resilient through transformation rather than restoration, stable yet brittle outside a narrow range, or changing without improving fit.

### E5.2 Adaptation Criterion

    AdaptationCriterion {
      candidate_system
      relevant_environment_or_condition set
      baseline fit relation
      change occurrence or process
      post_change fit relation
      fit criterion and measure
      causal relation between conditions and change
      retention duration
      costs and tradeoffs
      excluded alternative explanations
      required continuity inputs
      generalization boundary
      evidence and coverage
    }

    AdaptationAssessmentResult {
      environmental_change posture
      system_change posture
      causal_coupling posture
      baseline_and_later_fit posture
      retention posture
      tradeoff posture
      adaptation_result:
          ADAPTATION_ESTABLISHED
        | ADAPTATION_NOT_ESTABLISHED
        | ADAPTATION_UNRESOLVED
      coverage
    }

    ADAPTATION_ASSESSMENT(candidate, adaptation_profile, ES)
      -> EmergenceEvaluationEnvelope<AdaptationAssessmentResult> | ILL_TYPED

Feedback is neither necessary nor sufficient in every Profile, though it often participates. A change that merely follows an input may be response, not adaptation. A change that improves one metric while destroying a defining capacity may be maladaptation under a broader Criterion. Every fit measure exposes what and whose conditions it selects.

### E5.3 Robustness Criterion

    RobustnessCriterion {
      target function_or_organization
      disturbance class and range
      performance_or_integrity bounds
      interval
      resource conditions
      admitted degradation
      failure boundary
      evidence and coverage
    }

    RobustnessAssessmentResult {
      disturbance_class_and_range posture
      target_function_or_organization posture
      retained_performance_or_integrity posture
      admitted_degradation posture
      robustness_posture: CapacityPosture
      coverage
    }

    ROBUSTNESS_ASSESSMENT(candidate, robustness_profile, ES)
      -> EmergenceEvaluationEnvelope<RobustnessAssessmentResult> | ILL_TYPED

Robustness is range-bound. “Robust” without a disturbance class, performance bound, and interval is incomplete. Redundancy, buffering, modularity, diversity, and error correction may realize robustness; none establishes it by architectural presence alone.

### E5.4 Resilience Criterion

    ResilienceCriterion {
      target bearer_or_system
      defining organization_or_capacity
      disturbance class
      impairment criterion
      recovery_or_reorganization paths
      acceptable retained and transformed distinctions
      unacceptable losses
      recovery interval
      resource and support conditions
      repeated_disturbance posture
      required continuity inputs
      evidence and coverage
    }

    ResilienceAssessmentResult {
      disturbance_posture
      impairment_posture
      recovery_or_reorganization posture
      defining_organization posture
      loss_and_transformation posture
      repeated_range posture
      resilience_posture: CapacityPosture
      coverage
    }

    RESILIENCE_ASSESSMENT(candidate, resilience_profile, ES)
      -> EmergenceEvaluationEnvelope<ResilienceAssessmentResult> | ILL_TYPED

One actual recovery is Evidence of a recovery path, not automatically a general Resilience Capacity. An externally restored system can be resilient if the Profile includes that support relation; it cannot be called independently self-restoring when the external intervention is constitutive.

### E5.5 Self-Maintenance

A **Self-Maintenance Capacity** is the realized ability of an organized bearer to causally contribute, through its own processes and control relations, to preserving or renewing the organization and conditions required for its continued operation under a declared boundary and environment.

    SelfMaintenanceCriterion {
      system_boundary
      defining organization
      viability or operating variables
      system-produced maintenance processes
      external support and dependencies
      resource and waste flows
      repair and replacement relations
      control and regulation basis
      failure and ending conditions
      interval and continuity inputs
      evidence and coverage
    }

    SelfMaintenanceAssessmentResult {
