## E12 — Novelty, Creation, Problem Resolution, and Contribution

### E12.1 Novelty Is Relative

Novelty is a relation between a candidate and a comparison basis under a Domain, Interval, Resolution, and search or coverage method.

    NoveltyKind :=
        ORGANIZATIONAL_LEVEL_NOVELTY
      | TEMPORAL_ONSET
      | HISTORICAL_FIRST_WITHIN_CORPUS
      | COMBINATORIAL_NOVELTY
      | FUNCTIONAL_NOVELTY
      | OBSERVER_RELATIVE_NOVELTY

    NoveltyCriterion {
      candidate
      novelty_kind
      comparison_corpus_or_state_space
      Domain
      Interval
      ResolutionContext
      equivalence_and_similarity relations
      derivation_and_prior_source evidence
      search_method
      corpus_coverage
      threshold_or_distinction criterion
      evidence and uncertainty
    }

    NoveltyAssessmentResult {
      comparison_basis posture
      search_coverage posture
      equivalence_similarity posture
      derivation posture
      novelty_result:
          NOVEL_WITHIN_SCOPE
        | NOT_NOVEL_WITHIN_SCOPE
        | NOVELTY_UNRESOLVED
      novelty_kind
      coverage
    }

    NOVELTY_ASSESSMENT(candidate, criterion, ES)
      -> EmergenceEvaluationEnvelope<NoveltyAssessmentResult> | ILL_TYPED

“First ever” against an open or inadequately searched corpus is `NOVELTY_UNRESOLVED`. Novel to one observer is not historically first. Novelty does not imply usefulness, truth, quality, intelligence, creativity, authorship, Ownership, or value.

### E12.2 Creation, Production, Discovery, and Invention

- **Production** is an Occurrence that brings about an Artifact, State, Representation, or effect.
- **Creation** is production under a Criterion that includes relevant novelty, origination, or transformation.
- **Discovery** is an Occurrence through which a previously unrecognized existent, relation, pattern, or fact becomes evidenced or known; it does not create the discovered referent.
- **Invention** is production of a novel Artifact, method, organization, or use under a declared novelty and attribution basis.

The terms do not settle authorship, Ownership, patentability, credit, entitlement, or value. Those require their own institutional and legal relations.

### E12.3 Creative Capacity

A **Creative Capacity** is a realized ability to produce candidates that satisfy a declared novelty criterion together with any required fit, constraint, coherence, or usefulness criteria across a condition class.

    CreativeCapacityCriterion {
      bearer
      production domain
      novelty criterion
      constraint_satisfaction criterion
      variation and selection organization
      attribution and external assistance
      operating range
      repeated_manifestation and generalization requirements
      evidence and coverage
    }

    CREATIVE_CAPACITY_ASSESSMENT(candidate, profile, ES)
      -> EmergenceEvaluationEnvelope<CapacityAssessmentResult> | ILL_TYPED

Random generation can produce a novel item without establishing Creative Capacity. Imitation can participate in creativity. One novel output does not establish a general creative Capacity. A creative bearer need not be conscious unless the Profile requires an experiential aspect.

### E12.4 Problem and Resolution

A **Problem Specification** identifies an initial condition, desired or admissible outcome set, constraints, evaluation method, and Scope. A **Resolution Occurrence** is an actual Process producing an outcome that satisfies the specification. A **Resolution Capacity** is the ability to do so across a declared problem class.

    ProblemResolutionResult {
      problem_specification posture
      solver_and_process posture
      constraint_satisfaction posture
      outcome_verification posture
      side_effect_and_externality posture
      resolution_result:
          RESOLVED_WITHIN_SCOPE
        | NOT_RESOLVED_WITHIN_SCOPE
        | RESOLUTION_UNRESOLVED
      capacity_inference: CapacityPosture | NOT_APPLICABLE
      coverage
    }

    PROBLEM_RESOLUTION_ASSESSMENT(candidate_process, problem_specification, profile, ES)
      -> EmergenceEvaluationEnvelope<ProblemResolutionResult> | ILL_TYPED

A problem can be resolved technically while producing constitutional invalidity, external harm, unacceptable cost, or low value. Those assessments remain separate.

### E12.5 Contribution Family

Ontology separates Contribution Act, Contribution Occurrence, Contributed Content, Contribution Relation, Contribution Effect, Contribution Assessment, and Contribution Record. Emergence preserves every position.

    ContributionCriterion {
      contributor_candidate
      contribution_act_or_occurrence
      contributed_content_or_resource
      receiving_context_or_system
      target_process_or_work
      causal_or_constitutive path
      co_contributors and dependencies
      baseline_or_comparison method
      effect classes
      temporal horizon
      attribution method
      uncertainty and coverage
    }

    ContributionAssessmentResult {
      occurrence_or_action posture
      content_or_input posture
      receiving_context posture
      causal_or_constitutive path posture
      co_contributor posture
      effect posture:
          CONTRIBUTION_EFFECT_ESTABLISHED
        | CONTRIBUTION_EFFECT_NOT_ESTABLISHED
        | CONTRIBUTION_EFFECT_UNRESOLVED
      attribution posture
      assessment_dimensions
      coverage
    }

    CONTRIBUTION_ASSESSMENT(candidate, criterion, ES)
      -> EmergenceEvaluationEnvelope<ContributionAssessmentResult> | ILL_TYPED

Participation, effort, intention, authorship, origin, proximity, expense, sponsorship, visibility, or submission can coexist with contribution but does not establish its effect by itself.

### E12.6 Causal and Constitutive Contribution

A contribution can be:

- **causal**, where the occurrence changes an effect under the causal method;
- **constitutive**, where the input is part of what realizes the whole or work;
- **enabling**, where it supplies a condition necessary within the Scope;
- **preventive**, where it blocks an outcome;
- **corrective**, where it addresses an Error;
- **maintenance**, where it preserves a capacity or organization.

These modes may overlap. The Criterion must declare whether omission, substitution, marginal difference, necessity, sufficiency, or actual process tracing is used. No one counterfactual method is universally available.

### E12.7 Multi-Contributor and Synergistic Effects

Where several contributions jointly produce an effect:

- causal attribution may be shared, asymmetric, interaction-dependent, redundant, overdetermined, or unresolved;
- the sum of isolated marginal effects may not equal the joint effect;
- one contributor's necessity does not prove sole sufficiency;
- substitutability can vary over time and condition;
- an unresolved share must not be forced into a convenient percentage.

    ContributionAttributionVector {
      contributors
      contribution modes
      per_contributor evidence
      interaction terms
      substitution and necessity postures
      uncertainty
      method
    }

The vector is an assessment, not an Ownership table or payment schedule.

### E12.8 Impact

An **Impact Assessment** evaluates effects across selected bearers, Domains, criteria, horizons, and counterfactuals. Impact may be beneficial, harmful, mixed, uncertain, delayed, indirect, reversible, or irreversible.

    ImpactCriterion {
      effect references
      affected bearers and domains
      baseline_or_counterfactual
      horizon
      selected metrics and values
      externalities and displacement
      distribution
      uncertainty
    }

    ImpactAssessmentResult {
      effect_actuality posture
      affected_bearer_and_domain posture
      baseline_or_counterfactual posture
      distribution_and_externality posture
      impact_polarity: EmergencePolarity
      impact_extent_or_order
      result:
          IMPACT_ESTABLISHED
        | IMPACT_NOT_ESTABLISHED
        | IMPACT_UNRESOLVED
      coverage
    }

    IMPACT_ASSESSMENT(effect_set, impact_criterion, ES)
      -> EmergenceEvaluationEnvelope<ImpactAssessmentResult> | ILL_TYPED

Effect and impact assessment are not equivalent. A real effect can have unresolved importance. A claimed impact can lack causal support. Positive impact under one evaluator's Criterion can be negative under another's.

### E12.9 Origin and Source

Origin and source positions are historical or causal relations. They may support authorship or Contribution Claims under appropriate Criteria. They do not create permanent privilege, control, landlord status, Authority, superior moral worth, Ownership, rent, reward, or a larger value claim by identity.

Later constitutional or economic systems may lawfully recognize bounded compensation or other treatment. That treatment must arise through their own explicit rules and cannot be smuggled into Emergence as a natural entitlement.

### E12.10 E12 Closure

New to me is not first in Reality.  
Novelty is not creativity.  
Creation is not discovery.  
Resolution is not universal benefit.  
Participation is not Contribution Effect.  
Contribution is not sole causation.  
Impact is not value by identity.  
Origin is not landlordship.

